{"version":2,"kind":"Article","sha256":"fb8689a23fe6356a68bfcb4cb1d1272a42e679bc5dc7e2cd03f2cab3a8f61970","slug":"introduction","location":"/gnosticism/introduction.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"The Gnostic Story","authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Eric Araujo","given":"Eric","family":"Araujo"},"name":"Eric Araujo","id":"contributors-introduction-generated-uid-0"}],"numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"thumbnail":"/gnosticism-cover-2c7650eaa85b2842e89ea5fa40167283.png","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"introduction.md","url":"/introduction-7ae45209af27417cb2f9f55888feb948.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"“Error never shows itself in its naked reality, in order not to be discovered. On the contrary, it dresses elegantly, so that the unwary may be led to believe that it is more truthful than truth itself.” (Irenaeus of Lyons)","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"idZ9CFiFEf"}],"key":"VjO42UOJhb"}],"key":"M5Bdxp6UsR"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Introduction","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"JGuQG1SuD8"}],"identifier":"introduction","label":"Introduction","html_id":"introduction","implicit":true,"key":"q5iadf3QE8"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"At this part of the course, we are concerned about what is the Gospel in the context of the early church. We have already seen how the early Christians understood their mission in the Hellenistic culture through the writings of the Apostolic Fathers. Now, we will explore the challenges that the early church faced in defining and defending the Gospel message, particularly in the context of Gnosticism.","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"lH6ra359kr"}],"key":"oM9yCMDlaz"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In a context of polytheism, dualism, syncretism, and mystery religions, the early Christians had to articulate what they believed about Jesus Christ and his significance for salvation. Gnosticism, with its emphasis on secret knowledge (gnosis) and its dualistic worldview, posed a significant challenge to the early church’s understanding of the Gospel. Therefore, the church needs a story, a narrative that makes sense of who Jesus is, what he has done, and what it means for humanity. That is made through the creation of the creeds.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"PwRFm2m7Ee"}],"label":"gnosticism","identifier":"gnosticism","html_id":"gnosticism","key":"BtR6EQy9Bz"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Word About ","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"ujm30TRfYW"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sola Scriptura","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"cMveT04iGF"}],"key":"sSx4Aq253v"}],"identifier":"a-word-about-sola-scriptura","label":"A Word About Sola Scriptura","html_id":"a-word-about-sola-scriptura","implicit":true,"key":"OGF2usQj68"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sola Scriptura","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"EKez1JnflJ"}],"key":"Y3mKexNiE6"},{"type":"text","value":" is a theological doctrine that asserts that the Bible is the sole infallible source of authority for Christian faith and practice. This doctrine emerged during the 20th century, known as the “Scripture Alone” principle, and is based on the works from the Protestant Reformation in the 16th century as a response to certain practices and teachings of the Roman Catholic Church, which reformers believed were not grounded in Scripture.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"qaffsYzRP9"}],"key":"T2otb5cKtI"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is very relevant to reflect on the fact that Scripture Alone can be very misleading and even harmful if not properly understood. The early church did not have a New Testament canon as we have today. The canon was formed over several centuries, and the early Christians relied on oral tradition, apostolic teachings, and various writings that were considered authoritative at the time. Therefore, the early church had to rely on creeds, councils, and the guidance of the Holy Spirit to discern the true teachings of Christianity.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"TtVmEujyVi"}],"key":"KJ1B80h8i9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"If we are to take ","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"PktrSB0L2A"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sola Scriptura","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"gyduelTHAm"}],"key":"c1QKa2F6kC"},{"type":"text","value":" seriously, we need to understand that the Bible itself points us to the community of faith, the church, as the context in which Scripture is interpreted and understood. The early church’s creeds and councils were not attempts to add to Scripture but rather to clarify and articulate the essential truths of the Christian faith in response to various challenges and heresies.","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"iGOhvDqqAF"}],"key":"XW9guxZXVC"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Therefore, we must rely on more than Scripture alone. We must also rely on:","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"ZlAhjX5rn1"}],"key":"fbA3fcMhp0"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creed (the story, the narrative that makes sense of Scripture)","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"Z7DukooifV"}],"key":"hTtMKoev0e"}],"key":"go0dU6aYjw"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Canon (the authoritative books that make up Scripture)","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"dnAeGpcVaq"}],"key":"WPjou4HLfb"}],"key":"wTBZb5cmnQ"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Holy Spirit (the guide and teacher of the church)","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"CevEw40KEC"}],"key":"mAE6iSq6qR"}],"key":"V7fF4ZFIp2"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leaders (the teachers and pastors who help interpret and apply Scripture)","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"q6orCMbNT3"}],"key":"hzXwujZnGu"}],"key":"KVyLa0NsHI"}],"key":"WgaDgm7ti1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"If any one of these is missing or misunderstood, our understanding of the Gospel can be distorted. That could be seen in the medieval church, where the leaders abused their authority, leading to corruption and the need for the Reformation. It could also be seen in some modern churches that reject creeds and traditions, leading to a fragmented and individualistic approach to faith.","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"KAUV220fZH"}],"key":"xvHuRW4vNK"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gnosticism","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"gDUuESbTTx"}],"identifier":"gnosticism","label":"Gnosticism","html_id":"gnosticism-1","implicit":true,"key":"G9XYL2bVnT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gnosticism was a diverse and complex religious movement that emerged in the early centuries of Christianity. It was characterized by its emphasis on secret knowledge (gnosis) as the key to salvation and its dualistic worldview, which posited a stark contrast between the spiritual and material realms.","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"e3X8354pRC"}],"key":"j6Lfu4oGyg"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/gnosticism-cover-2c7650eaa85b2842e89ea5fa40167283.png","alt":"A traveller peers through an opening in the firmament in this illustration from Camille Flammarion’s L’atmosphère : météorologie populaire (Paris: Hachette, 1888), p. 163","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"oYHoTpbEky","urlSource":"/figures/gnosticism-cover.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A traveller peers through an opening in the firmament in this illustration from Camille Flammarion’s L’atmosphère : météorologie populaire (Paris: Hachette, 1888), p. 163","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"WQedsZAxEb"}],"key":"OVtI91GOh7"}],"key":"GHhM5qmseg"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"ifdSIzGZld"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gnostics believed that the material world was created by a lesser deity, often referred to as the ","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"JbK5SPkOuR"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Demiurge","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"iZ2vIalNmX"}],"key":"GBhySKr3ez"},{"type":"text","value":", who was distinct from the true, transcendent God. They viewed the material world as inherently evil or corrupt, while the spiritual realm was seen as pure and good. According to Gnostic beliefs, human beings contained a divine spark or spirit that was trapped within their physical bodies, and salvation involved awakening this divine spark through gnosis.","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"uxRzeYWhPs"}],"label":"demiurge","identifier":"demiurge","html_id":"demiurge","key":"SWRsW5rMO5"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gnosticism presented a significant challenge to the early Christian church, as it offered an alternative understanding of Jesus Christ and his mission. Gnostics often portrayed Jesus as a divine being who imparted secret knowledge to a select few, rather than as the incarnate Son of God who came to redeem humanity through his death and resurrection. This led to various Gnostic sects and teachings that diverged from orthodox Christian beliefs, prompting the early church to respond with creeds and councils to clarify the true nature of Christ and the Gospel message.","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"pJIADbcvu1"}],"key":"QQe2LUAUVx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Therefore, we can define Gnosticism by the following characteristics:","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"key":"KG6IIp8enx"}],"key":"saHjDR0Spk"},{"type":"table","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Characteristic","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"key":"PhomUFtxUf"}],"key":"HHZLBKLHDx"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Description","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"key":"FVtD5SxIWD"}],"key":"RxyR9s46DL"}],"key":"GbNB73NGug"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"metaphysical dualism","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"key":"cBCj6UI7Ys"}],"key":"SpyQnVOEO8"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Belief in two opposing realms: the spiritual (good) and the material (evil)","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"key":"t0TPqytGw1"}],"key":"oLjiaF4DnT"}],"key":"IJVPNGYp2c"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"material world is wrong/meaningless/evil","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"qN8sszzYdW"}],"key":"OJRvP03Cpi"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The physical world is created by a lesser deity (Demiurge) and is inherently flawed","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"J1zwivOV4I"}],"key":"jhQXj9L3Cc"}],"key":"pyQklHU0g5"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"escape/“salvation” through esoteric knowledge","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"quN6Cs71Iz"}],"key":"v7AO6cEcGL"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Salvation is achieved by acquiring secret knowledge (gnosis) that awakens the divine spark within","p